[s0l0!-header][h1]Classes in ES6[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

In this lesson we'll explain how to create a [b]class[/b] that can be used to create multiple objects of the same structure. 
A class uses the keyword [b]class[/b] and contains a [b]constructor[/b] method for initializing.

[s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" ][b]class[/b] Rectangle {
  [b]constructor[/b](height, width) {
    this.height = height;
    this.width = width;
  }
}[/code]
A declared class can then be used to create multiple objects using the keyword [b]new[/b].
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1653"]const square = new Rectangle(5, 5);
const poster = new Rectangle(2, 3); [/code]
[note]Class Declarations are [b]not hoisted[/b] while Function Declarations are. If you try to access your class before declaring it, [b]ReferenceError [/b]will be returned.[/note][b]
[/b]You can also define a class with a [b]class expression[/b], where the class can be named or unnamed.
A [b]named[/b] class looks like: [code format="js" codeId="1651"]var Square = class Rectangle {
  [b]constructor[/b](height, width) {
    this.height = height;
    this.width = width;
  }
};[/code]
In the unnamed class expression, a variable is simply assigned the class definition: [code format="js" codeId="1652"]var Square = [b]class [/b]{
  [b]constructor[/b](height, width) {
    this.height = height;
    this.width = width;
  }
};[/code]
[note]The [b]constructor [/b]is a special method which is used for creating and initializing an object created with a class.
There can be only [b]one [/b]constructor in each class. [/note]

