[s0l0!-header][h1]Class Methods in ES6[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

ES6 introduced a shorthand that does not require the keyword [b]function [/b]for a function assigned to a method's name. One type of class method is the [b]prototype method[/b], which is available to objects of the class.
[s0l0!-header][b]For Example: [/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1199"]class Rectangle {
  constructor(height, width) {
    this.height = height;
    this.width = width;
  }
  get [b]area[/b]() {
    return this.calcArea();
  }
  [b]calcArea[/b]() {
    return this.height * this.width;
  }
}
const square = new Rectangle(5, 5);
console.log(square.[b]area[/b]); // 25[/code]
[note]In the code above, [b]area [/b]is a getter, [b]calcArea [/b]is a method.[/note]
Another type of method is the [b]static method[/b], which cannot be called through a class instance. Static methods are often used to create utility functions for an application.
[s0l0!-header][b]For Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1200"]class Point {
  constructor(x, y) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
  }
  [b]static distance[/b](a, b) {
    const dx = a.x - b.x;
    const dy = a.y - b.y;
    return Math.hypot(dx, dy);
  }
}
const p1 = new Point(7, 2);
const p2 = new Point(3, 8);

console.log(Point.[b]distance[/b](p1, p2));[/code]
[note]As you can see, the static [b]distance [/b]method is called directly using the class name, without an object.[/note]
