[s0l0!-header][h1]Inheritance in ES6[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]extends[/b] keyword is used in class declarations or class expressions to create a child of a class. The child inherits the properties and methods of the parent.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="4902"]class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  speak() {
    console.log(this.name + ' makes a noise.');
  }
}
class Dog [b]extends[/b] Animal {
  speak() {
    console.log(this.name + ' barks.');
  }
}

let dog = new Dog('Rex');
dog.speak();[/code]
In the code above, the [b]Dog [/b]class is a child of the [b]Animal [/b]class, inheriting its properties and methods.
[note]If there is a constructor present in the subclass, it needs to first call [b]super[/b]() before using [b]this[/b]. Also, the [b]super[/b] keyword is used to call parent's methods.[/note]
For example, we can modify the program above to the following:[code format="js" codeId="4903"]class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  speak() {
    console.log(this.name + ' makes a noise.');
  }
}
}

class Dog [b]extends [/b]Animal {
  speak() {
    [b]super[/b].speak(); // Super
    console.log(this.name + ' barks.');
  }
}

let dog = new Dog('Rex');
dog.speak();[/code]
[note]In the code above, the parent's [b]speak() [/b]method is called using the [b]super [/b]keyword.[/note]

